;; amu's language ladder toward one typed source for kotoba-lang/browser
;; (ADR 0353, which decides ADR 0352).
;;
;; A floor is CLOSED only when its gate -- a `(deftest <gate>` in the floor's
;; repository (default kotoba-lang/amu) -- is on that repository's
;; origin/main. The superproject tick `scripts/amu-language-tick.cljk` reads
;; exactly that. The gate test must pin the admission AND the refusal the
;; floor keeps (ADR 0353 "What stays refused"). Order is the order the loop
;; takes them; an iteration may split or reorder a floor when measuring shows
;; the ladder was wrong, in the same commit, with the reason.
{:format :amu.language-ladder/v1
 :goal "kotoba-lang/browser as one typed source that amu compiles for the hosted engine and for x86_64-aiueos-kernel-v1 / aarch64-macos, without giving up safety, speed or code identity"
 :adr "0353"
 :floors
 [{:id :export-from-publicity :gate "export-derived-from-defn-publicity-test"
   :goal "a module's export vector is its public top-level definitions; defn- is private; an explicit :export still wins; the interface CID is computed from the public set"
   :amends "ADR 0005"}
  {:id :absence :gate "nil-field-is-absence-test"
   :goal "nil as a map-literal value makes the field [:option T] and absent; truthiness only on :bool and [:option T]; when / if-let / when-let / cond-> / some-> over an option desugar to option-match; strings and records are never truthy"
   :amends "ADR 0028"}
  ;; Split out of :absence (2026-09-25), because measuring showed it is not
  ;; the same change: the predicate primitives (`record-equal` was the one
  ;; measured) still answered the legacy 0/1 i64. Landed the same day at the
  ;; language surface, not through KIR: the six 0/1 operations (record-equal,
  ;; typed-map-equal, typed-set-equal, hetero-vector-equal, task-ready?,
  ;; object-cas-won) are elaborated to `(= op 1)` by kotoba-sema, so the KIR
  ;; operations and every backend are unchanged. Retiring the 0/1 KIR
  ;; answer itself is not needed for safety and is left to whoever measures a
  ;; cost in the extra compare.
  {:id :bool-predicates :gate "numbers-are-never-truthy-test"
   :goal "every predicate primitive answers :bool, not a 0/1 i64; an :i64 `if` / `when` / `if-let` test is refused by name; numbers are never truthy"
   :amends "ADR 0028"}
;; :row-records split in three (2026-09-25), because measuring showed three
  ;; changes in three places: inferring the row and monomorphizing the calls is
  ;; one kotoba-sema pass before parameter inference; the row OPERATIONS need
  ;; record types that are not declared schemas (assoc extends the row) and a
  ;; retirement of the keyword->i64 map that `{:a 5}` still lowers to; and a
  ;; specialization's CID derived from its generic's is amu's
  ;; definition-identity, which today hashes only monomorphic KIR (and
  ;; kotoba-hir's function schema refuses a key naming the generic).
  {:id :row-records :gate "row-polymorphic-record-inference-test"
   :goal "an unannotated parameter read as a record, or passed on to a row parameter, gets the row type {:k T | r} from those uses; its function exists only as its specializations, one per record type a call passes (monomorphized), each typed at that record; a non-record argument, a record lacking a field the row reads, an exported generic and a generic used as a value are refused by name"}
;; :row-operations split in two (2026-09-26), because measuring showed two
  ;; changes: the four operations are a type-directed elaboration in
  ;; kotoba-sema's `rewrite-record-projection` onto record-new / record-get /
  ;; record-assoc (nothing reaches KIR), while the rest is unification --
  ;; a map literal typed by the row it is passed to (today it lowers to the
  ;; keyword->i64 map before any row is known, and is refused as "map"),
  ;; `(if c m n)` joining two parameters, a loop helper made generic with its
  ;; function, and `(nth [(mk) (mk)] 1)` (refused "expected i64, got
  ;; [:ref :b/s]") -- each a change to inference, not to elaboration.
  {:id :row-operations :gate "row-operations-extend-and-shrink-test"
   :goal "assoc / dissoc / merge / select-keys are row operations whose results are record types: assoc extends, dissoc / select-keys shrink, merge assoc's a record's or a map literal's fields; a parameter a row operation uses is a row"}
  ;; :row-unification split in two (2026-09-26), because measuring showed two
  ;; changes in two places: a row argument, an if join and a loop helper are
  ;; all decided in kotoba-sema's row pass (`specialize-row-parameters`),
  ;; where the row is known; a vector literal of records and a let-bound map
  ;; literal are lowered by `desugar-expr` from their source alone, before
  ;; any type is inferred (`closed-vector-literal-type`,
  ;; `keyword-map-literal-record-fields`), and retiring the keyword->i64 map
  ;; changes the lowering -- and the definition CID -- of every all-i64
  ;; keyword literal, not only those a row receives.
  {:id :row-unification :gate "row-literal-join-and-loop-unification-test"
   :goal "a map literal passed to a row is that row's record; a parameter returned beside a row ((if c m n)) unifies into it, and two records at one join are refused by name; a loop over a row parameter is specialized with its function"}
  ;; :literal-typing (2026-09-26): kotoba-sema `type-literals` types a map /
  ;; vector literal once its items' types are known. A vector of records is
  ;; [:list T] (hosted); native refuses it by the typed-values gate, which is
  ;; :native-handles' vector handle.
  {:id :literal-typing :gate "record-vector-and-map-literal-retirement-test"
   :goal "a vector literal of records is an ordinary typed vector ((nth [(mk) (mk)] 1) is the record, today refused \"expected i64, got [:ref :b/s]\"); a keyword map literal is a record wherever it is written, including a let binding later passed to a row; the keyword->i64 map is retired"}
  {:id :specialization-identity :gate "specialization-cid-derived-from-generic-test"
   :goal "every row specialization's definition CID is a function of its generic's body and its type arguments only (not of the generic's name, its own name, the caller or the module), so two modules specializing one generic at one record share a CID, it is the hand-written twin's, and ADR 0300's compile-once cache holds across them; the generic, which never reaches KIR, is given no CID"
   ;; Amended 2026-09-27: first worded "derived from the generic definition's
   ;; CID". Measured: the generic has no KIR and so no CID, and the
   ;; monomorphic-KIR CID already satisfies the consequence; a CID keyed on
   ;; the generic would split a specialization from its hand-written twin.
   }
;; :function-values split 2026-09-27. Measured: the ADR 0352 internal error
  ;; on a stored closure was kotoba-sema lowering a record's fields as a lazy
  ;; seq outside the lambda counter's binding, not a missing type -- fixed as
  ;; :stored-closures. The same day measured a closure's static type is :i64
  ;; (a [:fn ...] result contract erases to it), so arithmetic and equality on
  ;; a closure are admitted; that is what :function-values still owes.
  {:id :stored-closures :gate "stored-closure-in-record-and-vector-test"
   :goal "a closure stored as a map-literal field or vector element is a program, not the ADR 0352 internal error: called out of the record or vector on the KIR interpreter and compiled for both native targets; its capability is in the caller's effect row; a stored closure is never truthy"}
  ;; :function-values split 2026-09-27 (second time). Measured: refusing
  ;; number operations and equality on a closure, and a fn literal in a
  ;; [:fn ...] field, are both decided by kotoba-sema's closure analysis
  ;; (`infer-closure-refinements`) and the field's contract; the closure word
  ;; stays the i64 handle KIR already has. A DECLARED effect row is a new
  ;; component of `[:fn ...]` (validate-value-type!, the contract lift and
  ;; the effect ceiling), not the same change -- floor :function-effect-rows.
  {:id :function-values :gate "function-value-in-record-test"
   :goal "a closure is a function, not a number: (+ f 1), (+ (make) 4) with make returning [:fn ...], (+ (:f r) 1) on a [:fn ...] field, any number operation and (= f g) are refused by name; a fn literal is admitted where a [:fn ...] record field is declared, lifted under that contract, and anything else there is refused; the closure stays the one-word (lambda-id, captures) handle, which KIR receives as :i64"}
  {:id :function-effect-rows :gate "function-type-declares-its-effect-row-test"
   :goal "[:fn ...] declares an effect row; a closure whose body exceeds the row of the contract it is lifted under is refused by name, and a caller's row is bounded by the rows of the function types it calls, not by the closures a module happens to lift"}
  {:id :code-units :gate "string-code-unit-primitives-test"
   :goal "string-code-unit-count, string-code-unit-at and subs over code units are primitives; count of a string stays refused"}
  {:id :hiccup-node :gate "hiccup-literal-is-node-adt-test"
   :goal "Node = Text | Elem tag attrs [Node] | Frag [Node] over ABI v8 [:ref q]; a hiccup literal desugars to it; shape tests over hiccup become an exhaustive variant-match"}
  {:id :allocation-budget :gate "allocation-charged-to-fuel-test"
   :goal "every constructor debits the 64-bit fuel ledger; the ADT node/depth ceilings are the caller's budget, not the constants 64 / 12"
   :amends "osaho adt-node-limit / adt-depth-limit"}
  ;; Split 2026-09-30 from :allocation-budget, which landed the debit on the
  ;; reference (kotoba.kir, the oracle and verifier authority). Each target
  ;; keeps its own counter: native charges entries and recur steps, ESM's
  ;; cell() debits the cells budget only. Moving native codegen moves every
  ;; aiueos kernel object, so it is its own change.
  ;; Split 2026-09-30 again: the hosted targets and native are different
  ;; changes. ESM's cell() and wasm32's fuel global are one emitter each;
  ;; native fuel is the entry prefix plus the loop's bulk pre-charge, which
  ;; kotoba-verifier re-derives, and it moves the aiueos kernel objects.
  {:id :allocation-budget-targets :gate "target-constructors-charge-fuel-test"
   :goal "restricted ESM (kotoba-script cell()) and wasm32 (the fuel global, vector literals kept in locals included) debit one fuel unit per constructor as kotoba.kir does: the price of allocating is the reference's on each, and a program that allocates past its budget traps (budget/fuel, unreachable) one unit short"}
  {:id :allocation-budget-native :gate "native-constructors-charge-fuel-test"
   :goal "x86_64-aiueos-kernel-v1 and aarch64-macos debit one fuel unit per constructor, the loop's bulk pre-charge and kotoba-verifier's fuel re-derivation included, so a program that allocates past its budget traps budget/fuel natively as on the reference; the aiueos objects that move are re-attested"}
  {:id :definition-admission :gate "admission-by-definition-graph-test"
   :goal "the 1 MiB bound moves from linked source bytes to per-definition size and closure count; cssom/layout.cljk (1,083,132 bytes) is admitted definition by definition"
   :amends "ADR 0005 linked-source bound"}
;; :native-handles split in three (2026-10-01), because measuring on amu
  ;; 7e05c456 showed three changes in three places. (1) A declared record
  ;; crossing a function boundary compiled natively and was refused at the
  ;; oracle, cause unknown-schema-reference: kotoba.kir/native-program kept
  ;; :schemas only for a recursive table, and [:ref q] survives lowering in a
  ;; signature -- osaho and kotoba-verifier, no codegen. (2) Row operations
  ;; natively: assoc of a field a record has is refused by the typed-values
  ;; gate (record-assoc not qualified, kotoba-native / kotoba.kir), assoc
  ;; extending and dissoc by the verifier's record projection through a let
  ;; operand (kotoba-verifier). (3) The rest: a [:list T] of records, closure
  ;; and record loop-helper parameters (refused today by kotoba-sema before
  ;; any target: "expected map" for a record loop slot, "g is not a builtin"
  ;; for a closure loop slot), exit 65 for a target refusal. map-new: both
  ;; ADR 0352 reproductions, (:window/x {:window/id 1 :window/x 10}) and the
  ;; filterv, now compile for both native targets (measured the same day);
  ;; that no form still lowers to it is (3)'s to show.
  {:id :native-record-boundary :gate "native-record-function-boundary-test"
   :goal "a declared record ([:ref q]) as a function parameter or result, and a row specialized at a declared schema, compile for x86_64-aiueos-kernel-v1 and aarch64-macos with the oracle verified: the schema table joins the native program when a signature names a reference, and kotoba-verifier re-derives that need; coercion, = over records, a record as an if test and a record entry result stay refused by name"}
  {:id :native-record-operations :gate "native-row-operations-test"
   :goal "the row operations compile natively: record-assoc is qualified by kotoba-native and kotoba.kir's native gate, and kotoba-verifier's record projection sees through a let operand (a row operation elaborates to (record-get T (let [..] (record-new T ..)) :f), refused today as runtime KIR record projection rejected, exit 65, hand-written too) and an if operand ((:a (if (= c 1) m n)), floor :row-unification's join, refused the same way)"}
  ;; (3) split again (2026-10-01, amu 2846b4c7): the loop slot is
  ;; kotoba-sema's alone and refused before any target, hosted included; a
  ;; record slot then compiles natively with nothing else moved, while a
  ;; [:list T] of records is refused natively by the typed-values gate
  ;; (typed-list-new not qualified) wherever it is written, loop or not.
  {:id :loop-slot-types :gate "loop-slot-record-list-closure-test"
   :goal "kotoba-sema types a loop slot like a let binding: a map-literal slot is its record (not :map), a vector literal of records a [:list T] (not the :i64 placeholder), a closure slot is callable (not an unknown operation) and is a closure to the :function-values refusals; record and closure slots compile for x86_64-aiueos-kernel-v1 and aarch64-macos; a slot recurred to another record, a coerced field, a record or closure slot as an if test, = over them, a list item as an i64, a closure slot as a number and a number for a closure slot stay refused by name"}
  {:id :native-handles :gate "native-record-vector-closure-boundary-test"
   :goal "kotoba-native qualifies a [:list T] of records (typed-list-new / typed-list-nth / count over one-word handles) natively, a loop slot included, and no form of the ladder lowers to map-new; the forms of every floor above but :hiccup-node's compile for x86_64-aiueos-kernel-v1 and aarch64-macos (target refusals exit 65, not 70)"
   ;; Amended 2026-10-01: first worded "retires map-new". Measured (amu
   ;; d44506ec): no map literal with fields lowers to it and both ADR 0352
   ;; reproductions are records, but the empty literal {} still does -- the
   ;; legacy keyword->i64 map, which match map patterns are written against.
   ;; That is a kotoba-sema change of its own: floor :empty-map-literal.
   ;; "Every floor above" was measured by compiling every program the
   ;; frontend-only gates admit (82) for both targets: the rest is refused
   ;; natively by design (handle equality, a record entry result), exit 65.
   ;; The hiccup node is the one floor whose forms stay refused natively: its
   ;; closed schema reaches the elem record's [:map :keyword :string] attrs,
   ;; and a typed map has no native representation -- floor
   ;; :native-hiccup-node, a change of its own (kotoba-native and osaho).
   }
  ;; Landed 2026-10-01 in osaho, kotoba-native and kotoba-verifier: a key is
  ;; a scalar native code compares by content (i64, bool, string, keyword);
  ;; keys / vals / entry-at stay hosted (entry order is not a native
  ;; operation). ADR 0353 has the measurements.
  {:id :native-hiccup-node :gate "hiccup-node-compiles-natively-test"
   :goal "a [:map K V] of one-word keys and values is a native handle type (typed-map-new / typed-map-get / typed-map-assoc / typed-map-dissoc over the word arena), so the hiccup Node -- whose elem carries [:map :keyword :string] attrs -- crosses a native function boundary and floor :hiccup-node's programs compile for x86_64-aiueos-kernel-v1 and aarch64-macos; equality over a map handle stays refused"}
  ;; Both measured 2026-10-01 while closing :native-handles; each is
  ;; kotoba-sema's and refused before any target, hosted included.
  ;; Landed 2026-10-02 in kotoba-sema (0632bed0); ADR 0353 has the
  ;; measurements.
  {:id :empty-map-literal :gate "empty-map-literal-is-typed-test"
   :goal "{} is not the legacy keyword->i64 map: (assoc {} :a 1) is the record {:a 1}, an empty literal with no row to extend is refused by name, and no form lowers to map-new on any target; match map patterns read records"}
  ;; Measured 2026-10-02 while closing :empty-map-literal: a row is inferred
  ;; from (:a m) but not from (get m :a), and a match map pattern projects
  ;; its scrutinee with get, so (defn- f [m] (match m {:a x} x :else 0)) is
  ;; refused "expression type mismatch: expected map, got i64" -- the
  ;; retired map's name. kotoba-sema's alone, before any target.
  ;; Landed 2026-10-02 in kotoba-sema (21e3a23f); ADR 0353 has the
  ;; measurements.
  {:id :row-get-and-match :gate "row-get-and-match-read-a-row-test"
   :goal "(get m :a) and (get m :a d) on an unannotated parameter read a row as (:a m) does, so a match map pattern over a parameter specializes per record -- an arm naming a key the record lacks does not match in that specialization; a get with a computed key on a row stays refused by name"}
  {:id :loop-slot-beside-row :gate "loop-slot-typed-beside-a-row-read-test"
   :goal "a [:list T] loop slot is typed whatever precedes the loop: (let [ys {:a 1}] (+ (:a ys) (loop [zs [(mk) (mk)] i 0] (if (< i 1) (recur zs (+ i 1)) (count zs))))) answers 3 (the loop answers 2), refused before \"count requires a bounded vector, ...; got :i64.\" while the same loop without the row read is admitted"}
  ;; :browser-text-edit split (2026-10-02), measured on kotoba-sema 965feb37 by
  ;; compiling browser.text-edit (browser 8671500) with the mechanical changes
  ;; only: a :kotoba branch for code-unit-at and for a code-unit-count helper
  ;; (count of a string stays refused: two answers), the nil guards over
  ;; values that are never absent dropped ((or n lo) on an i64, (or
  ;; (:text/selection state) ..) on a vector -- a number or vector is never
  ;; truthy). Each floor below is one refusal that remained, in the order met;
  ;; the multi-arity move-caret / move-to with a {} options map and {:keys
  ;; [extend?]} were not reached and are unmeasured.
  ;; Landed 2026-10-02 in kotoba-sema (531e89d8); ADR 0353 has the
  ;; measurements.
  {:id :let-rebinding :gate "let-rebinding-is-nested-shadowing-test"
   :goal "a let that rebinds a row parameter -- (let [state (normalize-selection state)] ..) -- leaves it a row (refused before: the parameter stayed :i64, \"only a record satisfies a row\"), and a name bound again in one let is the nested let that shadows it (refused before: \"duplicate let binding\"); a read after the rebinding is the new value's, a rebound name has its new type, a record is never truthy, parameters stay distinct"}
  ;; Landed 2026-10-02 in kotoba-sema (8d3320fc): the crash was
  ;; specialize-row-parameters' (contains? generics arg) once there are more
  ;; than eight row generics (a hash map) and arg is an integer literal (a
  ;; BigInt, which has no hash); either of move-caret / move-to is
  ;; text-edit's ninth. ADR 0353 has the measurements.
  {:id :cljs-literal-hash :gate "text-edit-analyzes-on-nbb-test"
   :goal "kotoba-sema analyzes browser.text-edit (the mechanical changes above, every function defn- but empty-state, a main driving it) on nbb without the internal error \"Cannot create property 'closure_uid_..' on bigint '0'\" -- ClojureScript hashing the bigint an integer literal is, the class kotoba-sema's run-tests.cljk names for desugar-case; not measured on the JVM; located by bisection to the module, not yet to a pass"}
;; Landed 2026-10-03 in kotoba-sema (7290e22e): a keyword key written
  ;; nil anywhere in the module (literal entry or assoc pair) is [:option T],
  ;; T the one type the module's non-nil values at that key spell; nil is its
  ;; none, a spelled value or a map literal its some. ADR 0353 has the
  ;; measurements. Native: construction, assoc and presence compile; the
  ;; present record's projection is floor :native-option-record (closed).
  {:id :absent-field-from-assoc :gate "nil-field-typed-by-the-module-assoc-test"
   :goal "{.. :text/composition nil} is [:option T] with T the value the module assoc's into that field ({:composition/text \"\"}, composition-start), and (assoc s :text/composition nil) its none; refused today \"map literal value at key :text/composition is nil ... nothing here says T\"; a field two assoc's give two types stays refused by name"}
;; Landed 2026-10-03 in kotoba-sema (275b40a0): row specialization ran
  ;; before parameter inference, so code-unit-count's string parameter was
  ;; still :i64 inside normalize-selection's specialization, whose result
  ;; then stayed :i64; each round now infers parameters first. ADR 0353 has
  ;; the measurements.
  {:id :rebound-row-argument :gate "text-edit-rebound-state-is-a-row-test"
   :goal "delete-backward's and delete-forward's (insert-text state \"\"), state rebound by (normalize-selection state), passes a record to insert-text's row parameter; refused today \"argument state to insert-text is i64, and parameter state of insert-text is the row {:text/caret T :text/selection T | r}: only a record satisfies a row\"; a number passed to a row parameter stays refused by that literal"}
  {:id :native-vector-field :gate "vector-field-in-record-compiles-natively-test"
   :goal "a record field holding an i64 vector (:text/selection [start end], typed :vector-i64) compiles for x86_64-aiueos-kernel-v1 and aarch64-macos; refused today at the typed-values gate (\"typed values currently require ... the qualified native one-word string/record/variant/option/result slice\") for (nth (:sel s) 1) and (assoc s :sel [..]) alike"}
  {:id :typed-wasm-min-max :gate "min-max-qualified-on-typed-wasm-test"
   :goal "max and min compile on wasm32-kotoba-v1 in a module with typed values (browser.text-edit's clamp); refused today \"typed Wasm operation is not qualified\" {:operation max}"}
;; Landed 2026-10-03 in kotoba-verifier (3e8134cd) and kotoba-native
  ;; (04a8e0e3): the record an option holds, defaulted to R, is R to the record
  ;; check, and a record in an option is boxed to one word. ADR 0353 has the
  ;; measurements. Measured 2026-10-03 while closing :absent-field-from-assoc: the
  ;; record inside an [:option R] -- (if-let [c (:text/composition s)]
  ;; (:composition/text c) ..) -- is refused on both native targets by the
  ;; verifier, "runtime KIR record projection rejected" (record-get's operand
  ;; is an option-value-of, which record-schema-of does not type), and a
  ;; declared [:option R] parameter read the same way is "machine IR
  ;; rejected: branch-value-shape-mismatch". Presence alone compiles.
  {:id :native-option-record :gate "option-record-projects-natively-test"
   :goal "the record an [:option R] holds is projected on x86_64-aiueos-kernel-v1 and aarch64-macos -- browser.text-edit's (if-let [c (:text/composition state)] (:composition/text c) ..) and a declared [:option R] parameter read the same way; refused today \"runtime KIR record projection rejected\" and \"branch-value-shape-mismatch\"; projecting a field R does not declare, and an option as a number, stay refused"}
;; Measured 2026-10-03 while closing :rebound-row-argument, on kotoba-sema
  ;; 275b40a0: text-edit with move-caret and move-to is refused at main's
  ;; (move-caret (empty-state) 1), "record-get without a type descriptor
  ;; requires a record value; got :i64" -- the specialization's result is not
  ;; inferable. Minimized: a {:keys [x?]} parameter is "expression type
  ;; mismatch: expected map, got i64", and the 2-arity's {} "an empty map
  ;; literal {} has no row to extend".
  {:id :options-map-argument :gate "options-map-destructures-as-a-row-test"
   :goal "move-caret's and move-to's options -- ([state delta] (move-caret state delta {})) and ([state delta {:keys [extend?]}] ..) -- are admitted, a key the map does not have read as absence; refused today \"record-get without a type descriptor requires a record value; got :i64\" at (move-caret (empty-state) 1), minimized \"expression type mismatch: expected map, got i64\"; a number as the options map, and an option used as a number, stay refused"}
;; Measured 2026-10-04 while closing :options-map-argument: text-edit with
  ;; move-caret / move-to answers on the reference, on both native targets
  ;; with empty-state private, and compiles on wasm32 -- the absent option a
  ;; destructured key would be is folded away. A literal nil the program
  ;; keeps, (let [x nil] ..), is still "unsupported typed Wasm expression"
  ;; on wasm32 (option-none); natively it compiles.
  {:id :export-signatures :gate "export-abi-from-clojure-source-test"
   :needs-a-human "how a Clojure-readable source states an export's ABI: browser.text-edit's public functions take state (a row: refused as an exported generic, which has one ABI) and text (unannotated, so :i64; the test passes strings: \"expected i64, got string\"), and the typed signature spelling [m [:ref q]] is not Clojure. Candidates measured as possible, not chosen: Clojure type hints (^String text) and an ns schema the hint names, or the export taking the record the module's one public constructor (empty-state) returns"
   :goal "browser.text-edit's public functions export one ABI each with the source still read by Clojure"}
  ;; Measured 2026-10-03 while closing :native-vector-field: text-edit's
  ;; insert/select/delete in ONE main exhaust the compiler host's stack inside
  ;; the KIR oracle under kbb's default stack (:host/stack-exhausted, nothing
  ;; sealed); one operation per program compiles on both native targets.
  {:id :browser-text-edit :gate "browser-text-edit-native-test"
   :blocked-by [:absent-field-from-assoc :rebound-row-argument :native-vector-field :typed-wasm-min-max :native-option-record :options-map-argument :export-signatures]
   :goal "kotoba-lang/browser's browser.text-edit, migrated only mechanically (defn-, nil's meaning, a :kotoba interop branch), compiles for both native targets and its hosted tests pass on amu's output"}
